<SiteLayout title="Module 15: Application Layer — fred.fish" description="Application-layer protocols define how applications exchange information. DNS, DHCP, web, email, and file-sharing services each solve a different communication problem." active="cisco">
<section class="source-lesson">
<div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
<header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 15" label="MODULE 15 / NETWORK APPLICATION SERVICES" /><h1>Connect the services.<br /><span>Follow the protocols.</span></h1><p>Application-layer protocols define how applications exchange information. DNS, DHCP, web, email, and file-sharing services each solve a different communication problem.</p></header>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Separate application protocols from transport</h2><p>Application protocols define message formats and procedures used by applications. They rely on transport-layer services such as TCP or UDP and network-layer addressing to communicate between hosts. A browser, mail client, or file-sharing application uses its protocol to request or deliver a service.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1263 · IMG-1213</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Resolve names and configure hosts</h2><p>DNS translates domain names to IP addresses so users and applications do not need to memorize numeric addresses. DHCPv4 automatically assigns IP configuration. Its four-message process is Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledgment (DORA). DHCP options can provide a subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS server addresses.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1221 · IMG-1204</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Recognize common application services</h2><p>HTTP and HTTPS support web communication. SMTP is used to send email, while POP3 and IMAP support mail retrieval. FTP transfers files, and SMB supports file and printer sharing in many local networks. The application protocol determines the exchange; TCP or UDP carries the transport data.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1213 · IMG-1249</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Application Layer</h2><p>Practice the key terms, procedures, and troubleshooting decisions from this module.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
</section>
</SiteLayout>
